feat(triage,purchases): rebuild the review queue, and let it fix what it shows

The queue screen had the right content in a shape nobody signed: a stack of
cards with every case expanded, which with twelve of them is twelve screens of
scrolling to reach the one you came for. It is now the master/detail of the
high-fidelity guide (`3d`) — a narrow list on the left, one case open on the
right — with a header that states how many are unresolved, how many were
resolved today and that pending work stays out of every total, the area filter
as a segmented control, and a sort that sorts. It is also no longer called
«Revisar esto»: it is «Para decidir», which says what is expected of the person
rather than what the system did.

Deciding is a three-step assistant, as the guide draws it: look at what the
portal brought, choose what to do, and confirm with the consequences stated.
Nothing in step three is estimated — the figures come from the case's payload
or from what the person just typed. No orange anywhere on this screen (RF-21).

The second half is what the queue could not do: **fix** an unreadable row.
Until now a row the portal published broken could only be given for reviewed,
so the invoice never entered any total nor the due-date calendar — warning
without allowing a repair is half a promise (Artículo II). Invoices and orders
can now be typed by hand from the case itself, and they enter marked as
MANUAL, because a datum a person typed is never shown as something the portal
published (Artículo I). Giving the row for reviewed is still there, for when
the paper is not at hand.

When the portal later publishes that same row, readable and different, neither
side wins on its own: the record is set aside — out of every total, like any
doubtful datum — and the difference is asked as a new case with both values
side by side. Answering it in favour of what was typed remembers the rejected
values, because the portal republishes its row twice a day and a question that
comes back after being answered teaches people to ignore the queue.

Backend: `origin` and `rejected_portal_values` on invoices and orders
(migration 0022), the `ManualEntryDisputed` event, the decision handler in
`purchases`, and `CaseList.resolved_today` counted on the shop's clock and not
on UTC's.

Frontend: ReviewHeader, CaseQueue and CaseDetail; the sidebar carries how many
cases are waiting and shows nothing at zero; Ventas moved into «Catálogo y
datos», above «Catálogo y precios».

Tests: six integration cases for the manual load and the dispute — including
that a number already registered leaves the case pending instead of closing it
with nothing entered — and three screen suites for the queue, the manual load
and the sidebar.
